Track 3: Gate Chatter in the Supercell
- Story & Context: Decades after the Point Lisas strike of 1989 left the bulk terminals abandoned, local fishermen along the mangrove coastline swear that whenever a violent green supercell descends on the gulf, the decommissioned gantry cranes still ring out with ghost rhythms against the container hulls. The myth solidified when a scrap scavenger stumbled upon Baptiste's discarded tape recorder inside an old compressor shack, its tape loop perpetually catching on an analog gate and spitting out delayed, flutter-tongued flute phrases that answered the deep horn rumble of passing offshore tankers. The recording remains the scene's ultimate artifact, concluding with a crackling, overdriven crowd shout through the squall: "The harbor forgets the captains, but the fog keeps every song."
